Experts have warned that an obsession(痴迷) with healthy eating can cause\mental health problems. A survey among mental health professionals found 90\% of\those surveyed, like in previous research, had seen patients with orthorexia nervosa\(ON). \clozeblank{35}

The survey found some patients cut out foods like fish, meat or dairy,\considering them “unclean”. Their fixation on “pure” foods leads to deficiencies(缺陷)\like iron-deficiency anaemia or vitamin B12 deficiency. For example, Dr Niket\Sonpal found that his patients didn’t consume enough calories, minerals, protein or\carbohydrates. Some patients even cut out entire food groups after the big dinners of\holidays. \clozeblank{36}

The term ON was coined in 1997 by Dr. Steven Bratman. He noticed patients’\extreme diets caused malnutrition, anxiety, and even death. \clozeblank{37} It poses a\significant challenge. Healthcare providers may not be trained to spot the signs easily,\and patients might not be aware their eating habits. As a result, diagnosis and\treatment are delayed.

\clozeblank{38} Healthcare providers need to be attentive to identify and deal with ON\in patients, providing support such as nutritional advice, psychological aid, and\diet-diversity education. The public needs to know the risks of overly strict eating.

\clozeblank{39} True well-being comes from a healthy relationship with food, not an obsession\with “clean” eating. By taking these steps, we can prevent mental health issues caused\by fixating on healthy eating and promote a culture where food brings both nourishment\and joy.
